"beating-DF.eps (https://arxiv.org/pdf/1308.6463)\nciteturn197view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 14; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1308.6463\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1472\nL0@P0: arXiv:1308.6463v1 [astro-ph.IM] 29 Aug 2013\nL1@P0: Mon. Not. R. Astron. Soc. 000, 1–13 (2013) Printed 29 September 2018 (MN LATEX style file v2.2)\nL2@P0: Detecting multiple periodicities in observational data with\nL3@P0: the multi-frequency periodogram.\nL4@P0: I. Analytic assessment of the statistical significance\nL5@P0: Roman V. Baluev⋆\nL6@P0: Central Astronomical Observatory at Pulkovo of Russian Academy of Sciences, Pulkovskoje shosse 65, St Petersburg 196140, Russia\nL7@P0: Sobolev Astronomical Institute, St Petersburg State University, Universitetskij prospekt 28, Petrodvorets, St Petersburg 198504, Russia\nL8@P0: Accepted 2013 August 23. Received 2013 July 23; in original form 2013 May 20\nL9@P0: ABSTRACT\nL10@P0: We consider the “multi-frequency” periodogram, in which the putative signal is mod\u0002elled as a sum of two or more sinusoidal harmonics with idependent frequencies. It is\nL11@P0: useful in the cases when the data may contain several periodic components, especially\nL12@P0: when their interaction with each other and with the data sampling patterns might\nL13@P0: produce misleading results.\nL14@P0: Although the multi-frequency statistic itself was already constructed, e.g. by\nL15@P0: G. Foster in his CLEANest algorithm, its probabilistic properties (the detection signif\u0002icance levels) are still poorly known and much of what is deemed known is unrigourous.\nL16@P0: These detection levels are nonetheless important for the data analysis. We argue that\nL17@P0: to prove the simultaneous existence of all n components revealed in a multi-periodic\nL18@P0: variation, it is mandatory to apply at least 2n − 1 significance tests, among which the\nL19@P0: most involves various multi-frequency statistics, and only n tests are single-frequency\nL20@P0: ones.\nL21@P0: The main result of the paper is an analytic estimation of the statistical significance\nL22@P0: of the frequency tuples that the multi-frequency periodogram can reveal. Using the\nL23@P0: theory of extreme values of random fields (the generalized Rice method), we find a\nL24@P0: handy approximation to the relevant false alarm probability. For the double-frequency\nL25@P0: periodogram this approximation is given by an elementary formula π\nL26@P0: 16W2\nL27@P0: e\nL28@P0: −z\nL29@P0: z\nL30@P0: 2\nL31@P0: , where\nL32@P0: W stands for a normalized width of the settled frequency range, and z is the observed\nL33@P0: periodogram maximum. We carried out intensive Monte Carlo simulations to show\nL34@P0: that the practical quality of this approximation is satisfactory. A similar analytic\nL35@P0: expression for the general multi-frequency periodogram is also given in the paper,\nL36@P0: though with a smaller amount of numerical verification.\nL37@P0: Key words: methods: data analysis - methods: statistical - methods: analytical\nL38@P0: 1 INTRODUCTION\nL39@P0: The raw time-series data that are obtained in astronomical\nL40@P0: or other observations often contain more than one periodic\nL41@P0: component. For example, in the field of the exoplanets dis\u0002covery, the fraction of known multi-planet systems is close to\nL42@P0: 20%, with about 1.3 planets per system in average (See The\nL43@P0: Extrasolar Planets Encyclopaedia at www.exoplanet.eu). Al\u0002though some may claim that 20% is still only a minor frac\u0002tion, the multi-planet systems are the objects which are most\nL44@P0: interesting for further investigations and most important for\nL45@P0: the associated theory work.\nL46@P0: However, the complicated character of the compound\nL47@P0: ⋆ E-mail: roman@astro.spbu.ru\nL48@P0: radial velocity variation that multiple planets induce on\nL49@P0: their host star, sometimes makes such data rather difficult\nL50@P0: for an analysis. It is well-known that multiple periodic vari\u0002ations can interfere with each other and with the periodic\nL51@P0: patterns of the non-uniform time series sampling. When we\nL52@P0: plot a traditional periodogram of such data, we may even\nL53@P0: discover that its maximum peak is unrelated to any of the\nL54@P0: real periodicities (even for data without any random noise at\nL55@P0: all). Such examples are given, for example, in (Foster 1995)\nL56@P0: and also in the teaching manual (Vityazev 2001). This obvi\u0002ously appears because the single-sinusoid signal model, that\nL57@P0: is implicitly used by the (Lomb 1976)–(Scargle 1982) peri\u0002odogram, as well as by its more advanced relatives, is inad\u0002equate when dealing with the data containing two or more\nL58@P0-1: periodicities. Note that we do not speak here of the over-2 R.V. Baluev\nL59@P1: tone harmonics that appear when a single non-sinusoidal\nL60@P1: periodic signal is involved; for that case the so-called multi\u0002harmonic periodogram (Schwarzenberg-Czerny 1996; Baluev\nL61@P1: 2009b) should be used. We address here a more general case\nL62@P1: when the frequencies of the harmonics are independent and\nL63@P1: unknown (not binded to any basic frequency).\nL64@P1: On itself, the definition of a periodogram that could\nL65@P1: take into account at least two or many periodicities at once\nL66@P1: is rather trivial (see Section 2). We do not claim any in\u0002vention rights on it: the earliest, to our concern, papers\nL67@P1: utilizing essentially the same multi-frequency statistic were\nL68@P1: (Foster 1995, 1996a,b). In these works, the multi-frequency\nL69@P1: test statistic is treated as a part of the Foster’s CLEANest\nL70@P1: algorithm. In fact, the “global” version of the CLEANest\nL71@P1: method implies the direct dealing with the multi-frequency\nL72@P1: periodogram plotted in a multi-frequency space (or at least\nL73@P1: in some representative portions of this space).\nL74@P1: The non-trivial problem that we address here is the esti\u0002mation of the false alarm probability (FAP) associated with\nL75@P1: the observed peaks of this periodogram. The FAP is neces\u0002sary to justify any claims of signal detection in the presence\nL76@P1: of the noise. For the multi-frequency periodogram the FAP\nL77@P1: characterizes the joint significance of an extracted group of\nL78@P1: periodicities, and it is different from the usual significances\nL79@P1: of individual periodicities, treated each on its own. We dis\u0002cuss this difference and the practical importance of the joint\nL80@P1: FAP in Section 3.\nL81@P1: Since the FAP is related to the distribution function\nL82@P1: of the periodogram maxima, we estimate it with the gener\u0002alized Rice method for random processes and fields, which\nL83@P1: allows to efficiently approximate to the relevant extreme\u0002value distributions. We first used this method for the Lomb\u0002Scargle periodogram in (Baluev 2008), and after that it ap\u0002peared very useful. In Section 4 we derive the FAP formula\nL84@P1: for the multi-frequency periodograms using our generalized\nL85@P1: method described in (Baluev 2013).\nL86@P1: In Section 5 we discuss some further generalizations of\nL87@P1: the basic multi-frequency periodogram, which are analogous\nL88@P1: to the ones already known for the Lomb-Scargle one. After\nL89@P1: that, in Section 6 we present the results of Monte Carlo\nL90@P1: simulations that we carry out to verify our analytic FAP\nL91@P1: estimations, while in Section 7 we compare the detection\nL92@P1: sensitivity of the single- and multi-frequency periodograms.\nL93@P1: Finally, in Section 8 we provide a demonstrative example\nL94@P1: showing the potential capabilities of the double-frequency\nL95@P1: periodogram.\nL96@P1: 2 MULTI-FREQUENCY PERIODOGRAM\nL97@P1: First of all, we should define the dataset that we deal with.\nL98@P1: Let it consists of N measurements xi, taken at the time ti,\nL99@P1: and having the uncertainty σi. For now we assume that σi\nL100@P1: are known accurately, although later we will also consider\nL101@P1: the more wide-spread case when only the weights wi ∝ 1/σ2\nL102@P1: i\nL103@P1: are known, while the values of σi are known only to a free\nL104@P1: multiplicative constant. The errors of xi are assumed Gaus\u0002sian and mutually independent, with the variances given by\nL105@P1: σ\nL106@P1: 2\nL107@P1: i\nL108@P1: .\nL109@P1: When deriving the definition of the classic Lomb-Scarge\nL110@P1: periodogram, it is assumed that the data contain only pure\nL111@P1: noise (the null hypothesis) or the noise as well as a signal\nL112@P1: (the alternative hypothesis). The classic Lomb-Scargle peri\u0002odogram is based on a simple sinusoidal model of the signal\nL113@P1: to detect:\nL114@P1: µ = A cos(2πf t) + B sin(2πf t), (1)\nL115@P1: where the parameters A and B are implicitly estimated by\nL116@P1: means of a linear regression. Now let us assume that the\nL117@P1: compound signal, that we expect to reveal in the time-series\nL118@P1: data, is representable as the following sum of n independent\nL119@P1: sinusoids:\nL120@P1: µ =\nL121@P1: Xn\nL122@P1: i=1\nL123@P1: Ai cos(2πfit) + Bi sin(2πfit). (2)\nL124@P1: The definition of the multi-frequency periodogram itself,\nL125@P1: based on (2), is analogous to the one for the Lomb-Scargle\nL126@P1: periodogram, based on (1). Here we slightly extend the for\u0002mulae of a general (but still single-frequency) linear peri\u0002odogram, given in (Baluev 2008). Now we have 2n unknown\nL127@P1: linear coefficients Ai and Bi. In fact, the frequencies fi are\nL128@P1: unknowns too, but we treat them separately since they are\nL129@P1: non-linear parameters. Let us rewrite the model (2) in the\nL130@P1: vectorial notation as\nL131@P1: µ(θ, f, t) = θ · ϕ(f, t),\nL132@P1: ϕ(f, t) = {cos(2πfit), sin(2πfit)}i=1,2,...,n,\nL133@P1: θ = {Ai, Bi}i=1,2,...,n, f = {f1, f2, . . . , fn}. (3)\nL134@P1: Now we can eliminate the linear parameters θ by means of\nL135@P1: the linear least-square regression (that is, to estimate them\nL136@P1: on the basis of the input data xi, ti, and σi). To do this, we\nL137@P1: must solve the following minimization task:\nL138@P1: χ\nL139@P1: 2\nL140@P1: (θ, f) = h(x − µ)\nL141@P1: 2\nL142@P1: i 7−→ min\nL143@P1: θ\nL144@P1: (4)\nL145@P1: Here we have borrowed from (Baluev 2008) the notation\nL146@P1: hF(t)i, which stands for the weighted sum of the values F(ti)\nL147@P1: with the weights wi = 1/σ2\nL148@P1: i\nL149@P1: .\nL150@P1: Since this χ\nL151@P1: 2\nL152@P1: function is quadratic in θ, the necessary\nL153@P1: minimization can be done elementary:\nL154@P1: min\nL155@P1: θ\nL156@P1: χ\nL157@P1: 2\nL158@P1: (θ, f) = hx\nL159@P1: 2\nL160@P1: i − hxϕi\nL161@P1: T\nL162@P1: hϕ ⊗ ϕi\nL163@P1: −1\nL164@P1: hxϕi,\nL165@P1: θ\nL166@P1: ∗ = arg min\nL167@P1: θ\nL168@P1: χ\nL169@P1: 2\nL170@P1: (θ, f) = hϕ ⊗ ϕi\nL171@P1: −1\nL172@P1: hxϕi, (5)\nL173@P1: where ‘⊗’ is the dyadic product of vectors (a ⊗ b is a ma\u0002trix constructed of the elements aibj ). By analogy with\nL174@P1: the Lomb-Scargle periodogram, the multi-frequency peri\u0002odogram, associated to the model (2), can be defined as\nL175@P1: the half of the maximum decrement in χ\nL176@P1: 2\nL177@P1: implied by (5):\nL178@P1: z(f) = 1\nL179@P1: 2\nL180@P1: \u0010\nL181@P1: hx\nL182@P1: 2\nL183@P1: i − min\nL184@P1: θ\nL185@P1: χ\nL186@P1: 2\nL187@P1: \u0011\nL188@P1: =\nL189@P1: 1\nL190@P1: 2\nL191@P1: hxϕi\nL192@P1: T\nL193@P1: hϕ ⊗ ϕi\nL194@P1: −1\nL195@P1: hxϕi (6)\nL196@P1: Since this z(f) is basically a test statistic, its large values\nL197@P1: indicate that the data probably contain a variation that can\nL198@P1: be expressed (or at least approximated) by the model (2).\nL199@P1: However, it must be remembered that large values of z(f)\nL200@P1: does not yet mean that all sinusoidal components of (2) are\nL201@P1: actually present. When the data contain only a single si\u0002nusoid at a given frequency f\nL202@P1: ∗\nL203@P1: , the function z(f) will be\nL204@P1: large when fi ≈ f\nL205@P1: ∗\nL206@P1: only for a single index i (for all fj with\nL207@P1: j 6= i). In fact, the multi-frequency periodogram can also\nL208@P1: reveal single periodicities too, although its detection power\nL209@P1-2: in this case would be smaller than in the single-frequencyMulti-frequency detection significance 3\nL210@P2: framework (see Sect. 7). When the data contain n period\u0002icities at f\nL211@P2: ∗\nL212@P2: i\nL213@P2: , the multi-frequency periodogram has a multi\u0002dimensional cross-like shape: large values at the orthogonal\nL214@P2: lines fi ≈ f\nL215@P2: ∗\nL216@P2: i accompanied by an especially high peak at the\nL217@P2: intersection point. In practice, this picture may be of course\nL218@P2: made more complicated, e.g. due to the aliasing.\nL219@P2: There are pecularities of (6) at the diagonals fi = fj ,\nL220@P2: where the model (2) becomes formally degenerate. This de\u0002generacy can be easily eliminated, however. For example, for\nL221@P2: the double-frequency case this can be done by means of an\nL222@P2: equivalent replace of the base ϕ by the following set:\nL223@P2: cos(π(f1 + f2)t) cos(π(f2 − f1)t),\nL224@P2: sin(π(f1 + f2)t) cos(π(f2 − f1)t),\nL225@P2: cos(π(f1 + f2)t) sin(π(f2 − f1)t)/(f2 − f1),\nL226@P2: sin(π(f1 + f2)t) sin(π(f2 − f1)t)/(f2 − f1). (7)\nL227@P2: It is not hard to check that an arbitrary linear combi\u0002nation of these new base functions can be transformed\nL228@P2: to the form of the original model (2) with n = 2, but\nL229@P2: now at the line f1 = f2 we have a non-degerate base\nL230@P2: {cos 2πf t, sin 2πf t, t cos 2πf t, t sin 2πf t}. This also means\nL231@P2: that a large diagonal value of z(f) indicates, in general, a\nL232@P2: modulated periodicity with a slowly varying amplitude and\nL233@P2: phase.\nL234@P2: It is possible to further generalize the definition (6)\nL235@P2: to deal with some underlying variation in the data that is\nL236@P2: deemed to always exist, even if the multi-periodic signal that\nL237@P2: we seek is absent. For example, in practice we must always\nL238@P2: take into account at least an arbitrary constant offset of xi.\nL239@P2: We will discuss the ways to further generalize the multi\u0002frequency periodogram in Section 5. Though some of these\nL240@P2: generalizations should be treated in practice as mandatory,\nL241@P2: we do not write down the extended definitions here. This is\nL242@P2: because the definitions here are mainly intended for the use\nL243@P2: in Section 4 below, where we need to deal with more simple\nL244@P2: formulae.\nL245@P2: The matrix hϕ ⊗ ϕi in (6) is the Fisher information\nL246@P2: matrix associated to the parameters θ. In the case of the\nL247@P2: Lomb-Scargle periodogram, the single non-diagonal element\nL248@P2: of this matrix could be made zero by means of choosing a\nL249@P2: suitable time shift. This simplified the final formula to a sum\nL250@P2: of two squared terms. In the case of double-frequency peri\u0002odogram this diagonalization is much harder. As explained\nL251@P2: e.g. in (Schwarzenberg-Czerny 1998), in the general case we\nL252@P2: may perform a Gram-Shmidt orthogonalization of the base\nL253@P2: ϕ in the sense of the scalar product (a(t), b(t)) = ha(t)b(t)i.\nL254@P2: After this procedure, the Fisher matrix will appear strictly\nL255@P2: diagonal, so that the periodogram (6) will be expressed as\nL256@P2: a sum of 2n squares. This orthogonalization must be done\nL257@P2: anew for each new set of frequency values.\nL258@P2: The main difference of the multi-frequency peri\u0002odogram (6) from the Lomb-Scargle one is in the number of\nL259@P2: its arguments: it depends on many frequencies, rather than\nL260@P2: on only one. Therefore, its visual representation is a multi\u0002dimensional field rather than a usual graph of a function of\nL261@P2: a single argument. The honest computation of z(f) already\nL262@P2: for n > 3 on a full multi-dimensional grid of fi is a chal\u0002lenge. However, in practice it might be enough to compute\nL263@P2: the multi-frequency periodogram only in the vicinities of a\nL264@P2: small number of candidate frequencies that are revealed as\nL265@P2: peaks on the single-frequency periodogram. As we have dis\u0002cussed above, the multi-frequency periodogram has no iso\u0002lated peaks; its peaks are located in the intersection nodes\nL266@P2: of the grid generated by the mentioned system of candidate\nL267@P2: frequencies.\nL268@P2: It is possible that more computationally fast FFT-like\nL269@P2: methods of evaluation of z(f) may be constructed, similar\nL270@P2: to the ones already developed for the Lomb-Scargle peri\u0002odogram and its other extensions (e.g. Palmer 2009). We\nL271@P2: leave this question without attention here, since we further\nL272@P2: focus only on the statistical characteristics of the multi\u0002frequency periodogram.\nL273@P2: 3 STATISTICAL ISSUES COMING FROM THE\nL274@P2: SIGNAL MULTIPLICITY\nL275@P2: The construction of the multi-frequency periodogram is not\nL276@P2: the main goal of our present paper. This task on itself\nL277@P2: is rather easy, and a statistic similar to (6) have already\nL278@P2: been introduced in the literature; e.g. it was suggested by\nL279@P2: Foster (1995) in his CLEANest algorithm. Remarkably, the\nL280@P2: “global” version of the CLEANest just utilizes the direct\nL281@P2: evaluation of our multi-frequency periodogram (possibly, in\nL282@P2: some restricted domains of the entire frequency space).\nL283@P2: The goal that we are trying to reach in our work here\nL284@P2: is the more rigorous treatment of the statistical significance\nL285@P2: of the periodicities that we extract from the data.\nL286@P2: In practice, a sequential approach is usually adopted\nL287@P2: to detect the periods in the data: plot a single-frequency\nL288@P2: periodogram, find a candidate period, ensure that it is sig\u0002nificant, remove the relevant variation from the data. This\nL289@P2: sequential approach has two weaknesses. The first issue ap\u0002pears because for n signal components we must carry out a\nL290@P2: complete multiple hypothesis testing procedure rather than\nL291@P2: to just test each of these n components individually. After we\nL292@P2: have obtained many of the periods, we have done many sta\u0002tistical decisions. This means that we have a proportionally\nL293@P2: larger chance to make a false detection, in comparison with\nL294@P2: the extraction of only a single variation. Therefore, even if\nL295@P2: each of the period extracted had its FAP at some tolerable\nL296@P2: level, say 0.01, the overall FAP for the whole set of the vari\u0002ations is definitely greater: e.g. ∼ 0.1, if we have claimed to\nL297@P2: detect ten periods. In the end, although each of these peaks\nL298@P2: have passed the settled FAP threshold individually, we are\nL299@P2: still unsure about the reality of all of them.\nL300@P2: The other issue of the sequential approach is that we\nL301@P2: make a rather implicit assumption that all periods that we\nL302@P2: have extracted before the given step do actually exist. The\nL303@P2: FAP of the next detected period does not involve the un\u0002certainty related to the very existence of these previously\nL304@P2: detected peaks. In practice, we often dealt with the case\nL305@P2: when the LS periodogram of the data contain two similar\nL306@P2: peaks that have only a moderate significance. Such peaks\nL307@P2: are not nececcarily aliases of each other, so each of them\nL308@P2: might represent a true periodicity. However, in the single\u0002frequency framework, we cannot rigourously evaluate their\nL309@P2: significance, because we cannot be entirely sure that one of\nL310@P2: these periods is true. If these peaks are similar to each other\nL311@P2: and pass the FAP threshold both, we may only conclude\nL312@P2: that at least one of the relevant periodicities exists, but to\nL313@P2-3: confirm the existence of the both, we have to make an unjus-4 R.V. Baluev\nL314@P3: tified assumption that either first or the second one is true.\nL315@P3: In the end, this leads to an incorrect FAP estimation.\nL316@P3: To solve the two issues described above we need a\nL317@P3: method of calculation of the cumulative significance for a\nL318@P3: group of the periods, in addition to the significances of the\nL319@P3: individual periods of this group. In general, the significance\nL320@P3: of a group may be greater as well as smaller than the indi\u0002vidual significances, because there are two counter-acting ef\u0002fects. First, the increased number of free model parameters,\nL321@P3: describing the group of periodicities, leads to larger noise\nL322@P3: levels, and this decreases the group significance. Secondly,\nL323@P3: the contributions from really existing variations are accumu\u0002lated when they are treated jointly, and this increases the\nL324@P3: group significance.\nL325@P3: For example, considering the case of two equal peri\u0002odogram peaks, four distinct outcomes are possible:\nL326@P3: (i) None of the peaks is significant individually, and they\nL327@P3: are insignificant as a couple too.\nL328@P3: (ii) The peaks are significant individually, but not as a\nL329@P3: couple.\nL330@P3: (iii) The peaks are insignificant individually, but they are\nL331@P3: significant as a couple.\nL332@P3: (iv) The peaks are significant individually, as well as a\nL333@P3: couple.\nL334@P3: The conclusions following from the cases I and IV are obvi\u0002ous: our peaks are just insignificant or just significant both.\nL335@P3: In the case II we would conclude that only one of the peaks\nL336@P3: probably exists, but there is no enough observational evi\u0002dence to confirm that both of them are true. If the peaks\nL337@P3: are equal, we cannot decide which of them is the true one.\nL338@P3: In the case III we would draw basically the same conclusion:\nL339@P3: at least one or even both of the periods probably exist. We\nL340@P3: still cannot claim for sure that both peaks are true, since\nL341@P3: their individual significances are not enough for that.\nL342@P3: We give a graphical illustration of these types of out\u0002comes in Fig. 1. In these plots we schematically show the\nL343@P3: uncertainty domains for the components amplitudes K1 and\nL344@P3: K2. For the sake of the simplicity, we adopt that the distri\u0002butions of Ki are uncorrelated and Gaussian, although this\nL345@P3: is far from the truth. In this illustration, to “detect” a sin\u0002gle component or the couple means to ensure that a value\nL346@P3: Ki = 0 for a given Ki is outside of the relevant uncertainty\nL347@P3: domain. Notice that the uncertainty segments for a single\nL348@P3: Ki are not just projections of the uncertainty circle for the\nL349@P3: couple (K1, K2), because of the different number of the de\u0002grees of freedom. The sizes of the two-dimensional error el\u0002lipse and the error box inferred by the single-dimensional\nL350@P3: uncertainties is variable and depends on the distributions\nL351@P3: shape, on the dimnensionality of the problem, and on the\nL352@P3: FAP levels involved. The geometric relastionship between\nL353@P3: these regions may be different, although the diameter of the\nL354@P3: circle is always greater than the side of the box.\nL355@P3: Of course, the cases II and III are mostly paradoxi\u0002cal. In the case II, the both variations are significant on\nL356@P3: themselves, but their joint significance is insufficient due to\nL357@P3: the dimensionality penalty. Here the circle encompasses the\nL358@P3: box entirely. In the case III, we can detect the components\nL359@P3: jointly, thanks to the accumulation of their contributions,\nL360@P3: but when we treat them individually, we deal with only a\nL361@P3: portion of this joint contribution, and this portion appears\nL362@P3: insignificant. Here the box and consequently the circle partly\nL363@P3: amplitude 2\nL364@P3: amplitude 1\nL365@P3: case I\nL366@P3: amplitude 2\nL367@P3: amplitude 1\nL368@P3: case II\nL369@P3: amplitude 2\nL370@P3: amplitude 1\nL371@P3: case III\nL372@P3: amplitude 2\nL373@P3: amplitude 1\nL374@P3: case IV\nL375@P3: Figure 1. The illustration of various types of the interaction\nL376@P3: between the single-dimensional individual uncertainties (shown as\nL377@P3: a square error box) and the two-dimensional uncertainty domain\nL378@P3: (shown as a error circle). See text for the detailed discussion.\nL379@P3: cover the axes Ki = 0, but the origin K1 = K2 = 0 is still\nL380@P3: outside of the circle.\nL381@P3: One may think that these subtle geometric and prob\u0002ability effects are rather insignificant and might be just\nL382@P3: neglected in practice. This might seem so in the two\u0002dimensional graphs shown above, but when the number of\nL383@P3: the frequency component grows, the role of these effects can\nL384@P3: become only more importaint. The dimensionality penalty\nL385@P3: increases, and the behaviour near the boundary of the uncer\u0002tainty domain becomes decisive (recall that the most of the\nL386@P3: volume of a highly-dimensional ball resides near its surface,\nL387@P3: rather than in its core).\nL388@P3: By this point, the reader might feel a bit confused, since\nL389@P3: the issue that we describe still may look rather fuzzy and\nL390@P3: foggy. Now he is prepared to look at it from yet another point\nL391@P3: of view. Foster (1996a,b) clearly explains how it is important\nL392@P3: to explicitly settle the null hypothesis of the signal detection\nL393@P3: task. This is the hypothesis that explains the data through\nL394@P3: a more simple model that does not contain the putative\nL395@P3: signal or some its portions. The main difficulty with this null\nL396@P3: hypothesis is that it frequently resides in the subconscious\nL397@P3: domain of a researcher’s mind, and is not explicitly realized\nL398@P3: even when it is pretty complicated. So, what null hypothesis\nL399@P3: we usually bear in mind when we claim “our dataset contains\nL400@P3: n periodic components at the frequencies f1, f2, . . . , fn”?\nL401@P3: A traditional detection algorithm, based on the sequen\u0002tial extraction of these components, assumes that the null\nL402@P3: hypothesis represents one of the following branches:\nL403@P3: (i) No signal at all, only a constant\nL404@P3: (ii) The signal contains a single component f1\nL405@P3: (iii) Signal = component f1 + component f2 and no more\nL406@P3-4: (iv) Signal = component f1 + component f2 + compo\u0002nent f3 and no moreMulti-frequency detection significance 5\nL407@P4: . . .\nL408@P4: (v) Signal = sum of n−1 components, f1 to fn−1 and no\nL409@P4: more\nL410@P4: However, this null hypothesis is obviously inappropriate, be\u0002cause it relies on a particular detection sequence: first ex\u0002tract f1, secondly f2, and so on. In such a case, our alter\u0002native hypothesis, which is a completion of the null one to\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn197view1 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.aanda.org/articles/aa/pdf/2020/03/aa36905-19.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1\nL0: Failed to fetch https://www.aanda.org/articles/aa/pdf/2020/03/aa36905-19.pdf: (403) Forbidden\n"
